.\"
.\" KILL(2) - Substrate Native Personality
.\"
.TH KILL 2 "January 2026" "Substrate OS" "System Calls"
.SH NAME
kill \- send signal to a process
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <signal.h>
.PP
.BI "int kill(pid_t " pid ", int " sig ");
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR kill ()
system call sends a signal to a process or process group.
.PP
If
.I pid
is positive, the signal
.I sig
is sent to the process with the ID specified by
.IR pid .
.PP
If
.I pid
equals 0, the signal is sent to every process in the process group
of the calling process.
.PP
If
.I pid
equals \-1, the signal is sent to every process for which the calling
process has permission to send signals.
.PP
If
.I sig
is 0, no signal is sent, but existence and permission checks are still
performed.
.SH RETURN VALUE
On success, zero is returned.
.PP
On error, \-1 is returned, and
.I errno
is set appropriately.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I sig
is not a valid signal.
.TP
.B ESRCH
The process or process group does not exist.
.TP
.B EPERM
Permission denied.
.SH NOTES
.SS Substrate Native ABI
.nf
Syscall Number: 37
Arguments:      pid, sig (on stack)
.fi
.SS Signals
Common signals include:
.BR SIGINT " (2),"
.BR SIGQUIT " (3),"
.BR SIGKILL " (9),"
.BR SIGTERM " (15),"
.BR SIGTSTP " (20)."
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR signal (2),
.BR sigaction (2),
.BR substrate (7)
.SH STANDARDS
POSIX.1-2001, POSIX.1-2008.
